"executive_summary": "This study investigates the role of sarco/endoplasmic reticulum calcium-ATPase (SERCA) in paramyxovirus replication, specifically focusing on Peste des petits ruminants virus (PPRV) and Newcastle disease virus (NDV). The authors demonstrate for the first time that SERCA is a critical host factor facilitating paramyxovirus replication. Using the SERCA-specific inhibitor Thapsigargin, they show that treatment at non-cytotoxic concentrations significantly reduces viral replication in Vero cells. Conversely, overexpression of SERCA2 rescues the inhibitory effect of Thapsigargin, confirming SERCA's supportive role. The study reveals that paramyxovirus infection induces SERCA expression, which can be blocked by Thapsigargin. Mechanistic investigations using time-of-addition and virus step-specific assays indicate that Thapsigargin primarily inhibits viral entry and disrupts the subcellular localization of viral proteins, causing them to remain in cytoplasmic foci rather than localizing to the cell surface for budding. Notably, the study reports a rare phenomenon where NDV, but not PPRV, acquires significant resistance to Thapsigargin after long-term passage (70 passages) in Vero cells. This suggests that viruses may evolve to bypass dependency on cellular factors targeted by inhibitors. The findings highlight SERCA as a potential novel target for broad-spectrum antiviral therapeutics, offering a strategy to combat viral resistance by targeting host factors rather than viral proteins. The work contributes to the understanding of paramyxovirus replication mechanisms and the development of host-directed antiviral strategies.",
